You can achieve this effect by using nested `span` elements for each word and category. Then, use CSS to style each category with a different color and underline style. Finally, use JavaScript to add the underline to each word.

Here is an example of the HTML code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Colorful Underline</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div id="sentence">
    <span class="category1"><span class="word">Hello</span></span>
    <span class="category2"><span class="word">world</span></span>
  </div>

  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

And the CSS code in `styles.css`:

```css
.category1 {
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-color: red;
}

.category2 {
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-color: blue;
}

.word {
  display: inline-block;
}
```

And the JavaScript code in `script.js`:

```javascript
const words = document.getElementsByClassName('word');

for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
  const word = words[i];
  const category = word.parentElement;

  word.style.textDecoration = 'inherit';
  category.style.textDecoration = 'underline';
}
```

This code will give you the desired effect of underlining each word in a sentence with multiple lines in different colors representing different categories.

You can achieve this by first checking if the element's bottom is not visible in the window using the getBoundingClientRect() method. Then, only trigger the scrollIntoView() method if the condition is met. Here's an example of how you can do this in React's hook:

```jsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';

const MyComponent = () => {
  const elementRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleScroll = () => {
      const elementBottom = elementRef.current.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
      const windowHeight = window.innerHeight;

      if (elementBottom > windowHeight) {
        elementRef.current.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' });
      }
    };

    window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div ref={elementRef}>
      {/* Your element content */}
    </div>
  );
};
```

In this code snippet, we first create a useRef() hook to reference the element that we want to check and potentially scroll into view. Then, we use the useEffect() hook to add a scroll event listener that checks if the element's bottom is not visible in the window. If the condition is met, we call the scrollIntoView() method with the 'smooth' behavior option to scroll the element into view. Finally, we return the JSX with the elementRef attached to the desired element.

