- Placing workers outside Canada in Canadian jobs does NOT make up a significant portion of their business.
Start the list from the most revenue."

model_response:
You can use the following function in C# to find duplicates in a collection:

```c#
public List<T> FindDuplicates<T>(IEnumerable<T> collection)
{
    List<T> duplicates = new List<T>();
    HashSet<T> set = new HashSet<T>();

    foreach (T item in collection)
    {
        if (!set.Add(item))
        {
            duplicates.Add(item);
        }
    }

    return duplicates;
}
```

You can call this function with your collection of items to find duplicates. It will return a list of items that are duplicates in the collection.

prompt:
"In angular I have a FormGroup:
form: UntypedFormGroup = new UntypedFormGroup({
    fieldname: new UntypedFormControl("", Validators.required),
});
I want to focus the first element on start."

model_response:
To focus the first element of the form on start, you can use the ViewChild decorator to grab a reference to the form element and then call the focus() method on the input element inside the form. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```typescript
import { Component, OnInit, ViewChild, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
import { FormGroup, Validators } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-your-component',
  templateUrl: './your-component.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./your-component.component.css']
})
export class YourComponent implements OnInit {

  form: FormGroup;

  @ViewChild('firstElement') firstElement: ElementRef;

  constructor() {
    this.form = new FormGroup({
      fieldname: new UntypedFormControl('', Validators.required),
    });
  }

  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.firstElement.nativeElement.focus();
  }

}

```

In your component HTML file, you can add a template reference variable to the first form input element like this:

```html
<form [formGroup]="form">
  <input type="text" formControlName="fieldname" #firstElement>
</form>
```

By using the ViewChild decorator to access the firstElement input element and calling the focus() method on the native HTML element, you can set the focus to the first input element when the component is initialized.

